Subject: [PATCH] feat(nemotron_h): add the Nemotron 3 model

sglang-jax has no nemotron_h, so NVIDIA-Nemotron-3-Super-120B-A12B-BF16 and
NVIDIA-Nemotron-3-Ultra-550B-A55B-BF16 don't serve. Adds the model, the
config that resolves its irregular layer stack, and the Mamba-2 backend that
owns the recurrent state.

models/nemotron_h.py builds one block per layer: a Mamba-2 mixer, a GQA
attention layer, a LatentMoE layer or a dense MLP. A block is one norm, one
mixer, one residual add, so the stack never repeats a body and can't be one
scan over layers. configs/nemotron_h.py reads layers_block_type or
hybrid_override_pattern, derives num_hidden_layers from its length, and hands
the runner the layer lists both cache managers size from. It also reads the
linear_attention and full_attention names stock transformers writes when it
saves a config. The layer field has no default, so the class sets
has_no_defaults_at_init and AutoConfig can format the config it builds
without constructing an empty one.

A write to num_hidden_layers on a built config cuts the block list to its
first entries, so --model-layer-nums and a num_hidden_layers entry in
--json-model-override-args build that many layers. A config.json whose count
disagrees with its block list still fails to load.

The attention layers carry no position encoding. Both published config.json
files ship rope_theta and partial_rotary_factor, the reference declares
neither and reads neither, and this port does the same. Order comes from the
Mamba-2 layers.

layers/attention/mamba/ holds the scan. mamba2.py folds a chunk of tokens
into one affine step and turns the chunk boundary states back into per-token
outputs. mamba2_backend.py cuts the packed token axis into a chunk grid that
never spans two requests, folds each request's cached state into its first
chunk, and restarts the scan there, so one scan carries every request in the
batch. The restart is a select rather than a multiply by a zero decay, and a
padding slot reads B as zero, so an inf or NaN in one request's state can't
reach the requests packed around it.

With --enable-recurrent-extra-buffer the backend writes the state a forward
leaves to the request's track slot as well as its running slot. The scheduler
ends every forward that crosses a track boundary on the boundary, and the
radix tree adopts the track slot as the state of that prefix.

dt is clamped to mamba_dt_limit, whose (0.0, inf) default neither published
config.json overrides. Megatron-LM trains with that pair, and NVIDIA's CUDA
kernels and vLLM serve with it. The torch paths floor dt at time_step_min,
0.001, instead: transformers in prefill, and NVIDIA's torch_forward in prefill
and decode. On the published weights 212 of Super's 5,120 Mamba-2 heads have
softplus(dt_bias) below 0.001, so that floor binds. The test's random weights
keep dt above it, so the comparison with transformers doesn't reach it.

The routed experts aren't gated: one up_proj, mlp_hidden_act, one down_proj,
and they run in a shared latent of moe_latent_size rather than at
hidden_size. The router reads the full-width token first, which is the order
the reference uses. EPMoE grows a `gated` flag and a relu2 activation for
that; a gated layer is unchanged. Under a MoE quantization config an ungated
layer quantizes its two matrices.

The experts get the QuantizationConfig ModelConfig resolves, and nothing else
that sits on the config. The depthwise conv kernel is a bare parameter, so a
linear quantization rule leaves it alone, and fc2_latent_proj gets the
experts' output split over the tensor axis, the layout a QuantizedLinear
requires. A pre-quantized checkpoint is refused at build time, since the
weight mapping reads no scale tensors.

The model refuses --ep-dispatch-algorithm, --init-expert-location and
--ep-num-redundant-experts before it builds. The engine builds expert-location
metadata only for a config that sets num_experts, and the weight mapping loads
every expert in checkpoint order, so those flags would change nothing.

The SSM state is [heads, mamba_head_dim, ssm_state_size], 64 by 128, not
square. RecurrentStatePool allocated head_dim twice, which every existing
caller reads as the same number because KDA and GDN run the two widths equal,
so a square slot holds half the state. It now reads head_k_dim for the
second, which leaves those callers alone. The pool also asserted that
num_k_heads divides the tensor axis, an axis it never shards; the assert now
covers the two segments of the conv buffer that do shard.
mamba_ssm_cache_dtype is float32, so both state slots take float32 while the
weights ship BF16.

GroupRMSNorm required the tensor axis to divide num_groups. Nemotron 3 runs 8
groups at a tensor parallel size of up to 32, so it grows a second layout
that sums a group straddling shards with one all-reduce of a [tokens, groups]
array. The upstream test that asserted the old error now checks that layout
against an fp64 reference at tp=8 over 2 groups, traces it at tp=16 over 8
groups, and checks that the tensor axis still has to divide hidden_size.

Tested against the HuggingFace transformers implementation on CPU in float32:
every layer, the final hidden state and the logits match to 1.2e-07 on a
single request, on a packed batch and through a decode step, on a four-way
tensor axis as well as a one-way one. Through the engine's own pools,
metadata and jit, a prefix hit that resumes from a track slot matches a fresh
prefill, and a poisoned request's inf and NaN stay out of the three requests
packed around it. A stack cut to three blocks through ModelConfig matches the
full stack at that depth, and each expert-placement flag stops the build.
